Cached incremental SPIR-V builds
================================

Source proposal: ShaderBuild.CachedIncremental.Proposal.md
Current behavior: ShaderBuild.Incremental.md
Status: Phases 1–6 complete; Phase 7 pending.

Goal and shared contracts
  Make expensive build work proportional to changed inputs and their dependents. Unchanged
  stages and variants skip import expansion, emission, compilation, and interface extraction.
  Cheap enumeration, registry resolution, content checking, and output verification may remain
  catalogue-wide. Preserve runtime shader semantics, binary naming, manifest compatibility,
  coverage validation, bounded concurrency, output leases, and coherent publication.
  The source proposal is authoritative. Reconcile changes to this plan against it.

Ownership and scope
  Retain the shared shader resolver, ShaderFileHashIndex, ShaderVariantCache,
  ShaderInterfaceExtraction, ShaderOutputLease, and ShaderBuildReceipt as the owning APIs.
  TinyPreprocessor owns dependency discovery, graph relationships, ordering, and cycle detection.
  SpirvBuild owns persistence, input validation, work selection, and cross-invocation reuse.
  Keep Program thin. Separate identities, dependency records, variant records, selection, and
  publication into clearly named responsibilities under ShaderBuildTool/Spirv; use domain
  directories as needed. Do not duplicate import parsing, graph discovery, contract resolution,
  or binding-layout rules. Apply workspace documentation/comment and method-region conventions.
  Excluded: distributed caching, file watchers, automatic eviction, runtime loading redesign,
  shader algorithm changes, and support for arbitrary live readers during publication.
  Explicit clean remains the cache reclamation mechanism.

Execution and completion rules
  Run all build and test tools through subagents, as required by workspace instructions.
  Leave completion markers unchecked until focused validation and a second review reconcile
  the increment against its linked proposal sections and prior contracts.
  Record concise results with the corresponding tasks; do not add unrequested audit documents.
  Source review, unit tests, integration tests, packaging checks, and timings are distinct evidence.
  No game launch or in-game visual acceptance is required. Headless validation must establish
  clean/incremental output equivalence and runtime manifest compatibility.

Design decisions and remaining verification
  - Catalogue/shared-model boundaries are specified in the proposal, Resolved implementation
    contracts / Project and declaration boundary; Phase 2 implements and validates the split.
  - Effective-contract and implementation identity inputs are inventoried in the proposal,
    Concrete identity and projection inputs; Phase 2 verifies their implementation.
  - TinyPreprocessor direct-neighbor semantics and isolated-root behavior are verified by
    ShaderDependencyGraphTests; Phase 3 implements persistent snapshots and selection.
  - Hard-link ownership and interruption recovery are specified in the proposal, Publication
    transaction and recovery; Phase 4 executes filesystem checks. Copy fallback remains required.
  - Existing compiler-cache migration: establish compatibility in Phase 6; accept a one-time
    cold population where identity equivalence cannot be demonstrated.

Phase 1: Establish dependency, identity, and publication contracts
  Outcome: foundational choices and verification fixtures are concrete enough to implement.
  Depends on: none.
  Proposal: Existing ownership and proposed layout; Identity boundaries; Expanded source record;
    Coherent publication and output reuse; Resolved implementation contracts.

  ☑ Inventory the existing build and project graph.
    ☑ Trace generated offline/runtime declarations, shared contract types, project references,
      ShaderBuildTool entry points, SpirvBuild invocation, and deployed asset-copy targets.
    ☑ Specify a dedicated offline catalogue data assembly with one-way model dependencies;
      avoid conflicting shared type copies and circular references. Retain the same generator
      and authoritative models for runtime declarations.
    ☑ Inventory actual inputs used by source preprocessing, emission/layout, compiler execution,
      reflection/packaging, and catalogue membership. Record the decisions here or in the
      source proposal before implementing their consumers.
  ☑ Verify installed TinyPreprocessor 0.4.0 / TinyAst.Preprocessor 0.4.3 graph semantics.
    ☑ Use a root-to-include-to-nested-include fixture to establish direct/transitive behavior of
      GetDependencies/GetDependents, canonical identities, isolated roots, order, and cycles.
    ☑ Confirm ShaderSyntaxTreePreprocessor returns the graph and ShaderSourcePreprocessor's
      current text-only return drops it; define the result boundary that will retain it.
  ☑ Define output transaction and input-snapshot invariants.
    ☑ Enumerate interruption points in staging, old-generation rename, new-generation install,
      and receipt publication. Specify deterministic recovery before another receipt check.
    ☑ Define immutable-file ownership for hard links, supported same-volume behavior, copy
      fallback, and downstream copying only after successful publication.
    ☑ Specify source/contract snapshot validation before commit and bounded retry or failure
      behavior when inputs change while processing.
  ☑ Completion gate: dependency APIs and compilation boundaries are verified; decisions cover
    every identity input and publication transition without weakening proposal guarantees.

  Evidence and decisions (second review and independent completion audit passed)
    - Source proposal, Resolved implementation contracts, records the inspected project graph,
      offline model/catalogue split, exact identity inputs, immutable preprocessing result,
      publication interruption/recovery table, and fail-without-retry input-snapshot policy.
    - ShaderBuildTool.Tests/ShaderDependencyGraphTests.cs: 3/3 focused tests passed against the
      installed TinyPreprocessor 0.4.0 / TinyAst.Preprocessor 0.4.3 bridge. Queries return direct
      neighbors; isolated graph order is empty while membership/ProcessedResources retain root.
      Canonical IDs, nested ordering, root callback exclusion, and cycle handling are verified.
    - Subagent command: dotnet test ShaderBuildTool.Tests/ShaderBuildTool.Tests.csproj --no-restore
      --filter FullyQualifiedName~ShaderDependencyGraphTests -p:UseSharedCompilation=false
      -p:GenerateRuntimeConfigurationFiles=false --tl:off -v:minimal
      Environment: normal runtimeconfig generation failed NETSDK1060 (null path1). Focused tests
      used an ignored bin runtimeconfig targeting Microsoft.NETCore.App 10.0.0. Production shader
      outputs were not built. Assembly-separation and filesystem recovery execution belong to
      their later implementation gates; this phase establishes their concrete contracts.

Phase 2: Separate catalogue data and establish stable cache identities
  Outcome: contract-only edits do not globally invalidate unrelated compiler results.
  Depends on: Phase 1.
  Proposal: Identity boundaries; Existing ownership and proposed layout.

  ☑ Implement the dedicated catalogue assembly and directional shared-model dependencies.
    ☑ Keep offline catalogue data out of global processing/compiler identities; preserve
      runtime generator behavior, authoritative declarations, resolver semantics, and output paths.
    ☑ Verify tool resolution uses the actual built target with inherited configuration/output
      properties; preserve the existing custom-output behavior.
  ☑ Implement deterministic, unambiguous, versioned identities.
    ☑ Separate preprocessing, emission/layout, compiler policy/binaries, reflection/packaging,
      effective variant contract, and catalogue membership identities.
    ☑ Derive implementation identities automatically from actual relevant implementation
      inputs, managed/native dependencies, and schema versions. Do not rely only on manually
      incremented constants. Conservative invalidation within a shared processing assembly is allowed.
    ☑ Share effective-contract projection with owning resolver logic. Cover source, stage kind,
      entry point, fixed/structural defines, active specialization declarations/defaults, binding
      layouts, selection information, and output association wherever required.
    ☑ Keep compiler-result keys tied to final emitted source, stage, entry point, compiler
      binaries, target, optimization, warning policy, and Debug information.
  ☑ Verify identity boundaries with focused tests.
    ☑ Change a catalogue contract and prove unrelated implementation/compiler identities stay
      stable while affected effective-contract identities change.
    ☑ Cover every inventoried emitter/extractor input; verify deterministic ordering.
    ☑ Establish that processing changes require source reevaluation but identical emitted source
      remains compiler-cache eligible, and extractor-only changes do not require compilation.
  ☑ Completion gate: project boundaries build correctly and identity tests prove both required
    invalidation and isolation; no catalogue-wide hash substitutes for per-variant contracts.

  Evidence (second review and independent completion audit passed)
    - Proposal Identity boundaries / Resolved implementation contracts -> ShaderBuildModel and
      ShaderBuildCatalog project split, ShaderContractProjection, ShaderBuildIdentities,
      shared compiler argument policy, and separate receipt/compiler keys.
    - ShaderBuildIdentityTests covers effective source/condition/specialization/binding inputs,
      deterministic ordering/culture, assembly ownership, membership/output association,
