Phase 3: Persist library dependency graphs and reusable processing records
  Outcome: validated records can identify reusable work before AST construction or reflection.
  Depends on: Phase 2.
  Proposal: Persistent records; TinyPreprocessor ownership; Migration and scope.

  ☑ Retain successful preprocessing metadata with expanded text.
    ☑ Preserve DependencyGraph and ProcessedResources through ShaderSourcePreprocessor.
    ☑ Capture graph snapshots from the library result using its resource/relationship APIs.
      Record canonical opaque ResourceId values; never infer paths or edges from import strings.
    ☑ Associate every resource with physical input and actual-read content hash through the
      existing resolver callback; explicitly capture the directly supplied root and cross-domain inputs.
  ☑ Implement versioned expanded-source records under the private cache tree.
    ☑ Include root, preprocessing identity, graph snapshot, transitive input hashes, expanded
      text digest/reference, and per-root provenance. Persist data, not live AST/library objects.
    ☑ Restore snapshots with AddResource/AddDependency, including isolated roots; use library
      queries and any required visited-set traversal for affected-root selection.
    ☑ Replace an affected root's complete snapshot after successful expansion; remove its stale
      edges without removing dependencies belonging to other roots. Do not persist partial failures.
    ☑ Keep graph mutation and queries in controlled planning; parallel workers receive immutable
      work/snapshots. Any derived ownership lookup is not a second persisted dependency authority.
  ☑ Implement versioned variant and interface records.
    ☑ Variant records associate source/contract/emission identities, emitted digest, compiler key,
      binary digest, and interface key. Interface records include binary digest, relevant contract,
      extractor identity, configuration, payload integrity, and schema.
    ☑ Validate referenced artifacts before reuse. Restore missing/invalid interfaces from verified
      binaries without requiring compilation. Support equivalent-source compiler reuse.
    ☑ Constrain references to owned cache paths; publish atomically and retain per-key alias
      synchronization. Report local misses for malformed, incomplete, incompatible, or corrupt data.
    ☑ Retain historical successful content-addressed entries while current membership alone
      determines output ownership; cache entries are not successful catalogue receipts.
  ☑ Verify graph and record behavior.
    ☑ Round-trip graph snapshots, isolated roots, nested/shared/cross-domain dependencies,
      canonical IDs, cycles, removed edges, and ownership shared by several roots.
    ☑ Test failed preprocessing, missing resource associations, tampered record metadata/payloads,
      invalid paths, partial records, and concurrent aliases.
    ☑ Prove restoring valid records needs no preprocessing, AST emission, or reflection.
  ☑ Completion gate: record APIs validate all relevant identities/artifacts and preserve library
    graph semantics; they are ready for selection without enabling an unsafe receipt shortcut.

  Evidence (second review and independent completion audit passed)
    - Proposal Persistent records / Resolved implementation contracts -> ShaderExpandedSource,
      ShaderInputObservation and ShaderDependencySnapshot retain successful library topology,
      processed membership, and actual-read byte hashes; ShaderDependencyPlanner replaces roots
      independently and uses library queries for affected-root selection.
    - ShaderExpandedSourceCache, ShaderVariantRecordCache and ShaderInterfaceCache validate
      identities and referenced artifacts through atomic ShaderRecordStore envelopes. Existing
      ShaderVariantCache remains binary owner; PackagedInterfaceValidation owns payload schema.
    - 30 new tests cover graph round trips, UTF-16 byte hashing, cross-domain/shared/isolated
      roots, removed edges, cycles/failures, invalid references, partial/tampered records,
      concurrent aliases, equivalent emitted-source compiler reuse, and interface-only repair
      from actual compiled binaries. Warm restores avoid AST work and reflection.
    - Full ShaderBuildTool.Tests passed Debug 139/139 and Release 139/139. Final focused
      verification after the source-invariant ownership cleanup passed 30/30 in each configuration.
      Final runs reported zero warnings/errors. Commands: dotnet test
      ShaderBuildTool.Tests/ShaderBuildTool.Tests.csproj [-c Release] --no-restore
      -p:UseSharedCompilation=false -nr:false -m:1 --tl:off -v:minimal.
      Focused runs selected ShaderSourceRecordTests, ShaderRecordStoreTests and
      ShaderVariantRecordTests. Logs: artifacts/investigation/phase3/tests-debug.log,
      tests-release.log, focused-debug.log and focused-release.log.
    - Filename-format correction: all compiler and processing record keys now encode the full
      SHA-256 digest as 52 uppercase, unpadded Base32 characters using the shared encoder.
      Runtime variant names retain 26 characters; integrity metadata remains hexadecimal.
      Full Debug and Release suites passed 149/149 each with zero warnings/errors. Logs:
      artifacts/investigation/phase3/base32-debug.log and base32-release.log.
    - Source proposal and current-behavior documentation distinguish the reusable record APIs
      from selective catalogue integration. No receipt shortcut or output membership authority
      was added; publication recovery and normal-loop selection retain their later gates.

Phase 4: Preserve coherent publication while reusing unchanged outputs
  Outcome: complete generations can reuse binaries and recover deterministically after interruption.
  Depends on: Phases 1–3. The current build path can exercise this before selective integration.
  Proposal: Coherent publication and output reuse; Build algorithm failure guarantees.

  ☑ Implement recoverable generation publication under ShaderOutputLease.
    ☑ Restore the previous complete generation or finish a fully validated pending generation
      using explicit recovery state before receipt evaluation. Validate all recovery paths as owned.
    ☑ Keep the full manifest/binary generation coherent; never update changed active files
      individually. Publish the success receipt last and invalidate success on failed builds.
    ☑ Prune removed outputs only on successful publication and copy downstream only after success.
  ☑ Reuse unchanged verified binaries when constructing a generation.
    ☑ Hard-link immutable same-volume files where supported; replace files on updates rather
      than modifying linked contents. Preserve timestamps for unchanged deployed outputs.
    ☑ Exercise and report copy fallback when hard links are unavailable; correctness cannot
      depend on support. Write changed binaries and changed manifests as new files.
    ☑ Skip generation replacement entirely when verified membership, binary bytes, and manifest
      are unchanged; update only required private records/receipt.
    ☑ Repair damaged published outputs from verified cached results before publication.
  ☑ Verify failure and filesystem behavior.
    ☑ Inject cancellation/failures around every transaction transition, including receipt write;
      run restart recovery and verify no false success or accepted mixed generation.
    ☑ Cover simultaneous writers, hard-link immutability, fallback copies, retained timestamps,
      missing/corrupt outputs, removed membership, and the no-change publication path.
  ☑ Completion gate: recovery and rollback tests pass; publication preserves coherent output
    and documents the existing lack of arbitrary-live-reader transaction guarantees.

  Evidence (second review and independent completion audit passed)
    - Proposal Coherent publication / Resolved publication transaction -> ShaderPublication,
      ShaderPublicationJournal, ShaderPublicationPaths and ShaderGenerationSnapshot implement
      versioned recovery, exact-generation validation, confined cleanup and receipt-last commit.
      Program recovers under ShaderOutputLease before receipt evaluation or clean. Input checks
      retain consumed source observations and captured catalogue/processing/compiler identities.
    - ShaderBinaryReuse exercises actual Windows hard links and verified copy fallback; unchanged
      binaries/manifests retain timestamps. Identical generations skip directory replacement.
      ShaderVariantBuild now passes complete generations to the publisher; verified compiler
      cache results repair damaged outputs through the same path. Recovery trees are excluded
      from receipt membership and downstream asset copying.
    - 34 publication tests cover all eight transition checkpoints, first publication, cancellation,
      ordinary rename/receipt failures, rollback, corrupt/ambiguous generations, invalid journals
      and paths, actual link immutability, forced copies, no-change publication, nested paths and
      input rejection at both commit checks. Existing lease/incremental tests retain concurrent
      writer exclusion and real compiler cache/output repair coverage. Two additional receipt
      regressions cover malformed paths and nested shader directory ownership.
    - Full ShaderBuildTool.Tests passed Debug 180/180 and Release 180/180; final focused
      publication/incremental/lease/receipt checks passed 55/55 each, and final receipt regressions
      passed 12/12 each. All final runs reported zero warnings/errors. Commands use dotnet test
      ShaderBuildTool.Tests/ShaderBuildTool.Tests.csproj [-c Release] --no-restore
      -p:UseSharedCompilation=false -nr:false -m:1 --tl:off -v:minimal, with focused filters.
      Logs and isolated copy-probe receipts: artifacts/investigation/phase4.
    - Actual CopySpirvArtifactsToOutputAssets MSBuild target ran three times against isolated
      outputs: only active binaries/manifests copied, recovery/cache trees excluded, unchanged
      timestamps retained, and removed binary pruned. No game deployment or visual claim.
      Current-behavior documentation records recovery and the lack of arbitrary-live-reader
      transaction guarantees. Selective processing remains the next integration gate.

Phase 5: Integrate selective processing and truthful work counters
  Outcome: normal incremental builds skip expensive work for unrelated stages and variants.
  Depends on: Phases 2–4.
  Proposal: Build algorithm; Required behavior; Diagnostics.

  ☑ Integrate selection into the existing owning pipeline with thin Program orchestration.
    ☑ Recover under the lease, generate deterministic shared inputs, resolve registry, and validate
      source coverage. Do not replace generated includes when bytes are unchanged; retain atomic
      generation for concurrent configurations.
    ☑ Compute hashes/identities once through ShaderFileHashIndex; include recorded cross-domain
      dependencies. Preserve metadata-assisted mode and strict verifyContents semantics.
    ☑ Accept the complete receipt shortcut only with compatible schema, membership, inputs, and
      verified published outputs. Old receipts cannot bypass establishing new records.
    ☑ Select valid records before expansion/emission. Expand only invalid roots, once per equivalent
      shared source input; independently select variants with changed contracts/identities.
    ☑ Emit selected variants, reuse verified compiler results, compile misses with bounded concurrency,
      and reuse valid interface metadata. Restore binaries or metadata locally where possible.
    ☑ Validate the complete manifest assembled from reused/new records and exact membership;
      check consumed input/contract snapshots before committing through Phase 4 publication.
    ☑ Abort or retry inconsistent snapshots; preserve successful immutable cache entries after
      other failures without retaining a current success marker.
  ☑ Add operation counters at the owning operations and meaningful progress output.
    ☑ Count roots reused/expanded, variants reused/emitted, compiler reuse/actual invocations,
      interfaces reused/extracted, and outputs retained/replaced/repaired/removed.
    ☑ Distinguish variants from unique compiler jobs and account for equivalent-input aliases.
      Report immediate reuse and progress over selected work; skipped variants are not compilations.
    ☑ Retain compiler failure diagnostics and reasons; report dependency/schema/identity misses.
      Preserve summary compatibility or document exact replacement semantics.
    ☑ Separate input-check, selected-processing, and publication wall times from accumulated
      concurrent work. Include fallback publication costs without dumping unchanged records.
  ☑ Verify end-to-end selection with focused fixtures and operation counts.
    ☑ Warm unchanged: zero expansions, emissions, compilations, and extractions.
    ☑ Root/direct/nested/shared/cross-domain/import-topology edits: only affected roots and variants
      processed; unrelated records and timestamps retained. Missing imports fail safely.
    ☑ Contract-only, binding, specialization, structural/fixed define, entry-point, stage, policy,
      and configuration changes: all required work selected with unrelated work skipped.
    ☑ Extractor-only change: refresh interfaces using verified binaries. Equivalent source and
      reverted edits: compiler cache reuse where valid.
    ☑ Added/removed roots, stages, variants, and registry scopes: exact membership and pruning;
      unregistered owned roots still fail coverage checks.
    ☑ Inputs changed during processing and timestamp-preserving edits under strict verification:
      no receipt may claim a snapshot different from the processed input.
  ☑ Completion gate: selected processing and counters prove actual skipped work, with output
    equivalence and failure guarantees preserved through the integrated path.

  Evidence (second review and independent completion audit passed)
    - Proposal Build algorithm / Required behavior / Diagnostics -> ShaderBuildInvocation owns
      leased recovery, registry resolution, shared identity/hash capture and receipt-last publication.
      ShaderSourcePlan uses persisted TinyPreprocessor graphs to select affected roots; source and
      variant records are checked before AST work. ShaderVariantProcessor deduplicates compiler
      jobs by emitted input and reuses independently validated interface records.
    - Compatible version-2 receipts require complete reusable processing records, exact current
      generation membership and verified outputs. Consumed cross-domain inputs participate in
      fingerprints. Captured source/catalogue/implementation inputs are checked at commit boundaries;
      strict hash epochs reread each distinct input. Identical generated include bytes preserve
      timestamps under a cross-process mutex and atomic replacement.
    - Counters distinguish source/variant/interface work, actual compiler invocations and aliases,
      and retained/replaced/repaired/removed binaries. Progress covers selected emission jobs.
      Current-behavior documentation specifies compatibility-summary semantics and wall versus
      accumulated work timing. Compiler stage mapping follows declared kind, not asset suffix.
    - Full ShaderBuildTool.Tests passed Debug 222/222 and Release 222/222. Final focused Debug
      selective/identity/record/hash checks passed 72/72 after compiler-stage ownership cleanup.
      All final runs reported zero warnings/errors. Commands: dotnet test
      ShaderBuildTool.Tests/ShaderBuildTool.Tests.csproj [-c Release] --no-restore
      -p:UseSharedCompilation=false -nr:false -m:1 --tl:off -v:minimal.
      Logs: artifacts/investigation/phase5/tests-debug.log, tests-release.log and final-debug.log.
    - Focused real-compiler fixtures establish warm zero-work counts, root/direct/nested/shared/
      cross-domain and added/removed-import selection, contract changes, source reversion, clean
      output equivalence, alias counts, extractor-only repair, membership pruning, entry/stage
      rejection, supported target-policy changes, strict preserved-metadata edits and commit
      verification epochs. Registry-resolution and missing-input failures invalidate success
      while preserving the prior generation. The independent audit found no remaining discrepancy.
    - Actual policy testing exposed a pre-existing pinned dotnet-shaderc 1.2.2 limitation:
      warnings-as-errors is unsupported and successful warnings are omitted by the CLI. The
      unsupported flag now fails explicitly before processing; failure preservation is tested.
      No compiler upgrade or unsafe diagnostic-based emulation was introduced.
    - Migration/package qualification and matched production timing remain the subsequent gates;
      these focused receipts do not claim production latency or in-game effects.

Phase 6: Qualify migration, repair, packaging, and compatibility
  Outcome: existing users and all supported build configurations can safely use the new pipeline.
  Depends on: Phase 5.
  Proposal: Migration and scope; Validation and acceptance; Required behavior.

  ☑ Establish migration behavior for receipts and every new record schema.
    ☑ Populate complete graph/interface records before allowing warm reuse. Validate all referenced
      artifacts; reuse old compiler entries only with proven compatible identity, otherwise cold-fill.
    ☑ Verify first-upgrade, subsequent unchanged, single-edit, and reverted-edit behavior.
    ☑ Remove obsolete readers, compatibility scaffolding, or temporary instrumentation introduced
      during implementation once unnecessary; retain only explicitly documented required migration.
  ☑ Exercise missing/corrupt binary, record, manifest, and receipt recovery.
    ☑ Cover absent data and mismatching/malformed metadata; repair locally when possible and
      verify that unverifiable compiler results cause compilation rather than false cache hits.
    ☑ Repeat compilation/reflection failure and cancellation through the integrated pipeline.
  ☑ Verify compatibility and packaging.
    ☑ Cover Debug/Release isolation, registry scopes, custom output paths, writer leases,
      strict verification, explicit clean overriding reuse, and historical-cache retention.
    ☑ Preserve runtime naming and packaged interface schema/consumer compatibility; verify private
      graph, variant, interface, cache, and temporary files are not packaged.
    ☑ Verify deployed asset copying preserves unchanged timestamps and prunes obsolete binaries.
    ☑ Compare clean and incremental binaries/manifests under identical inputs and controlled paths,
      including reverted edits. Account for Debug source-path attribution in comparison fixtures.
  ☑ Completion gate: migration cannot bypass new dependencies, repair cannot bypass integrity,
    and supported build/package paths preserve runtime contracts without temporary scaffolding.

  Evidence (second review and independent completion audit passed)
    - Proposal Migration and scope / Validation and acceptance -> ShaderMigrationRepairTests
      verifies missing, malformed, incompatible-schema and digest-mismatched records for every
      processing family; local compiler metadata repair; receipt/manifest recovery; legacy
      hexadecimal-key cold population followed by warm reuse, single edit, reversion and clean.
      Comparisons include complete runtime binary/manifest bytes at identical Debug-visible paths.
    - Integrated reflection failure retains successful compiler artifacts without publishing
      success; cancelled invocations preserve the prior generation and resume safely. Existing
      compilation-failure, strict hashing, registry-scope, lease, runtime interface and publication
      recovery tests remain part of the full qualification suite. The obsolete per-file cache
      publication helper was removed; its hard-link test now exercises the actual cache Store API.
    - Full ShaderBuildTool.Tests passed Debug 251/251 and Release 251/251; focused migration/repair
      passed 29/29. All final runs reported zero warnings/errors. Commands: dotnet test
      ShaderBuildTool.Tests/ShaderBuildTool.Tests.csproj [-c Release] --no-restore
      -p:UseSharedCompilation=false -nr:false -m:1 --tl:off -v:minimal.
      Logs: artifacts/investigation/phase6/tests-debug.log, tests-release.log, focused-debug.log.
    - Actual isolated SpirvBuild builds passed for Debug and Release with custom OutputPath and
      SpirvArtifactsDir. GetTargetPath resolved the configuration-specific built tools. Runtime
      names remain equal, metadata identifies the correct configuration, and manifest/interface
      schema remains version 2. Production assets and deployed game outputs were not modified.
    - Actual CopySpirvArtifactsToOutputAssets target passed first-copy, unchanged and pruning
      checks in both configurations: only active binaries/manifests copied; source/variant/interface
      records, caches, work files and pending/previous trees excluded; unchanged timestamps retained.
      Receipts/logs: artifacts/investigation/phase6/custom-{debug,release}.log,
      target-path-{debug,release}.log, copy-{debug,release}-receipt.json and configuration-receipt.json.

Phase 7: Measure production behavior and complete documentation
  Outcome: matched production evidence demonstrates skipped work and an actual improvement.
  Depends on: Phase 6.
  Proposal: Current behavior and measured baseline; Validation and acceptance; Diagnostics.

  ☐ Repeat the copied-production-assets cold, unchanged, and single-SSAO-edit experiment.
    ☐ Match tool configuration, catalogue, concurrency, edit, and environment; retain production
      sources/outputs. Refresh a comparable baseline if the historical setup has drifted.
    ☐ Report internal timings and full command wall time separately; do not sum concurrent work
      as elapsed time. Historical wall times were 35.498 s cold, 0.458 s unchanged, and 14.716 s
      for one SSAO edit, with 435 hits and one compiler invocation on the edit run.
    ☐ Verify one affected root and only its affected variant are processed; all unrelated variants
      skip expansion/emission/reflection. Derive counts from current registry rather than hard-coding
      historical 436/435 totals. An already-cached emitted input may need zero compiler invocations.
    ☐ Require measured improvement over a matched baseline and elimination of unrelated expensive
      operations; do not claim an unmeasured fixed latency or GPU/in-game performance improvement.
  ☐ Run an end-to-end MSBuild check covering catalogue/tool rebuilding and deployed asset copying.
    ☐ Include a contract-only change to prove the assembly identity boundary holds through actual
      project builds, plus unchanged and isolated source edits and focused output equivalence checks.
  ☐ Update docs/ShaderBuild.Incremental.md and relevant shader authoring/build instructions.
    ☐ Explain dependency graph ownership, persistent records, identity boundaries, cache migration,
      counters, strict hashing limitations, repair, clean, publication recovery, and hard-link fallback.
    ☐ Reconcile proposal and plan links with final implementation and concise measured receipts;
      keep proposed behavior distinguishable from completed evidence.
  ☐ Completion gate: focused tests, integration/package checks, second review, and matched
    measurements support the claimed behavior; all proposal requirements are accounted for.

Final acceptance
  ☐ Reconcile every source-proposal requirement with the implemented work and evidence above;
    any unresolved change/deferment remains explicit and cannot silently weaken acceptance.
  ☐ Confirm source processing uses TinyPreprocessor graph capabilities without duplicate discovery.
  ☐ Confirm ordinary single-shader/contract edits skip unrelated expensive operations and runtime
    binaries/manifests remain equivalent to clean builds.
  ☐ Confirm failure recovery, migration, packaging, and supported configurations satisfy their gates.
  ☐ Confirm documentation, cleanup, review, and measured performance results are complete before
    marking the overall plan complete. Creating this plan does not begin implementation.
